ralph/codex-output/iteration-000048-20260519-010141.log:1114:- Operations artefact verification leaf (BE-0409 pattern, internal/controlplane/build/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, api_dockerfile_test.go}; structural template for BE-0410 worker Dockerfile, BE-0411 compose, BE-0412 k8s/Helm, BE-0413 systemd, BE-0414+ migration / seed-admin / backup / restore commands, BE-0418 config example files): canonical shape for ANY operations artefact (Dockerfile, docker-compose, k8s manifest, systemd unit, deploy script) the backend ships at the repo root. One Go test file per artefact in this package (api_dockerfile_test.go, worker_dockerfile_test.go, compose_test.go, ...), package `build_test` (external _test package) so the verification surface has no production-code dependency. Tests locate the artefact via repoRoot(t) which walks up from os.Getwd() until it finds go.mod — hard-coded relative paths break when the test moves. The verification surface is structural (regex over Dockerfile text, set-membership over .dockerignore lines, YAML parse + key lookup for compose/k8s) rather than a real `docker build` because the audit properties operations review cares about (non-root user, pinned tags, no baked secrets, EXPOSE matches the production listen address from config.ProfileProduction's apiAddr default) are textual, not behavioural. Forbidden-secret list MUST track config.Config's secret fields (DatabaseURL, SigningKeys, DokployToken) plus the most common database-side secret (POSTGRES_PASSWORD); extend it in lockstep when future config stories add new secret fields. ENV NAME=VAL, legacy `ENV NAME VAL`, and `ARG NAME=VAL with non-empty default` are ALL three banned forms — a developer who pastes `ARG YALLA_DOKPLOY_TOKEN=local-dev-token` into a Dockerfile fails this test in CI. Distroless static-debian12:nonroot is the canonical runtime image for any CGO_ENABLED=0 Go binary in this repo (UID/GID 65532, no shell, no package manager); HEALTHCHECK is intentionally omitted because the image has no curl/wget/sh — orchestrators probe /healthz and /readyz over HTTP directly. The BuildKit cache mounts (`--mount=type=cache,target=/go/pkg/mod` and `/root/.cache/go-build`) require the `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7` directive at the top of the file; without that line the syntax silently falls back to legacy and the cache mounts become no-ops. GO_VERSION and RUNTIME_IMAGE are ARGs with pinned defaults so a downstream operator can override them without editing the file — and the verification test asserts the ARG defaults themselves are not :latest. The .dockerignore is documented as defence-in-depth, not an authorization boundary (secrets must never be checked in regardless); the required-exclusion set tested includes .git, .env, secrets/, *.pem, *.key. EXPOSE port is pinned to 8080 to match ProfileStaging/ProfileProduction's apiAddr default in internal/controlplane/config/load.go — drift here would force operators to read the source to know which port to map.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000048-20260519-010141.log:1119:- Operator-driven health probe (BE-0039 pattern, internal/controlplane/backup/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, health.go, health_test.go} + httpapi/routes.go GET /healthz/backup + docs/operations/backup-restore.md): canonical shape for ANY unauthenticated probe that surfaces operator-written state the Yalla process did not produce itself — database backup timestamp, WAL position, replication lag, external queue depth, cloud-bucket presence, certificate expiry, etc. Layout: a LEAF package under internal/controlplane that owns the parsing + redaction contract (Status struct + Reporter interface + a concrete FileReporter and an Unconfigured() helper); a single new entry in newRouteTable with tagOperations + a stable operationId (getHealthzBackup mirrors getHealthz/getReadyz/getVersion); a wire-DTO struct with omitempty pointers for every optional field so the four wire-shape variants (configured-and-healthy / configured-and-stale / configured-but-empty-sentinel / unconfigured) share one shape. Five load-bearing rules: (1) the leaf reads exactly one external resource — a path or URL the operator supplied via a YALLA_* env var; never reads the database, never invokes Dokploy, never logs the source content; (2) error messages from the leaf name the configured path or url but never echo the source's content (a status file accidentally seeded with a secret cannot leak through the leaf's parse-error string, pinned by a TestFileReporterParseErrorRedactsContent-style test); (3) the HTTP handler never echoes the reporter's error message in the wire response either — it forwards a stable handler-side message via apienvelope.WriteErrorStatus(yerr.CodeUnavailable, ...) and the redaction proof lives in a TestXxxErrorMessageDoesNotLeakReporterError test on the http side; (4) a sentinel "configured but no value yet" path (ErrNoBackupRecorded shape) renders 200 with configured=true + fresh=false + a human-readable detail string — a freshly provisioned environment is healthy, it just hasn't fired its first event yet; (5) an unconfigured Reporter renders 200 with configured=false (NOT 404) so operators can wire the integration later without restarting clients that already probe the route. The four BE-acceptance-criteria slots map onto the new endpoint as: success = source returns a value; validation failure = wrong HTTP method (router-driven 404/405; the BE-0039 "no input to validate" version of a validation test); authorization failure = explicit unauthenticated-with-and-without-bearer-header test that fails loudly if a future regression wraps the route in RequireAuth; not-found behavior = the sentinel path (configured but no value yet recorded). Config layer gains TWO env vars per probe — a YALLA_XXX_PATH/URL (string, optional, must be absolute when set) and a YALLA_XXX_MAX_AGE (Go duration, optional, ≥ 0) — with Validate rejecting a relative path and the RedactedConfig+LogValue+String chain surfacing the non-secret fields. NewHandler grows ONE positional port (Reporter backup.Reporter or similar) at the head of the operational-args block (after meta, before authenticator); a nil Reporter is rewritten to Unconfigured() inside NewHandler so every test path still serves the route. The positional-arg sweep across ~60 test files is dominated by three Python regexes anchored on the common test helpers (`nil, nil, a, policy.NewEngine()` literal-a, `nil, nil, AuthIdent{}, policy.NewEngine()` struct-literal-auth, multi-line `nil, nil, a,\n\t\tpolicy.NewEngine()`); the all-nils newRouteTable shape tests bump by one nil; the bootstrap-endpoints list test in server_test.go adds the new path. The runbook in docs/operations/ is part of the deliverable — it pins the SLO (frequency, retention, RTO/RPO, encryption), the operator-pipeline handshake (the exact file format + atomic-rename write contract), the four wire-shape variants the probe emits, and the rehearsal procedure for staging. Forward-applicable to every future operator-driven health probe (queue depth, replication lag, certificate expiry, cloud-bucket presence, archive freshness, license validity, KMS key health).
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000048-20260519-010141.log:1121:- Pure orchestrator / workflow package = reconcile-shaped vertical (BE-0033 pattern, internal/controlplane/migrateimport/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, types.go, importer.go, importer_test.go}, the second instance of this shape after internal/controlplane/reconcile): canonical shape for ANY workflow that ingests external state, classifies it, and writes through ports — Dokploy import, drift reconciliation, billing reconciliation, compliance scanning, lifecycle teardown, etc. Package layout: doc.go (boundary + plan/apply + dry-run + redaction sections), AGENTS.md (conventions), types.go (every port interface PLUS every value type those interfaces reference — Scanner/Repository ports, Snapshot/Existing*/Create*Input value types, OwnerAssignment, PlanItem and Plan with stable JSON tags because the Plan IS the dry-run yalla.output.v1 payload; ItemStatus + ItemReason as the closed classification surface), importer.go (Config with Scanner + Repository + Logger + Now + Redactor; New() returns apierr.Internal on a missing port; Plan(ctx, in) is pure and never writes; Apply(ctx, plan) is best-effort and collects per-item failures in Result.Failures with redacted error strings; classify*() helpers are unit-testable in isolation; wrap{Scanner,Repository}Err() preserves catalogued apierr.* codes and upgrades untyped errors to apierr.DokployUnavailable (scanner) / apierr.StoreUnavailable (repository) / apierr.Internal (defensive contract assertions); a resolvedParents map seeded with the org-root entry lets the apply loop resolve child-after-parent in a single pass through the hierarchy-ordered items). The test file houses an in-package fakeScanner + fakeRepo with concurrent-safe mutexes and call recording, and covers: construction port validation (one nil per case → CodeInternal); Plan input validation (every required field, every kind/format check → CodeInvalidInput with the named field recoverable via apierr.ViolationsOf); the dry-run-doesn't-query-Repository contract (no-assignment Plan never calls the repo); every classification Status × Reason combination as a positive case (StatusReady / StatusSkipDuplicate / StatusSkipMissingParent / StatusSkipUnsupported / StatusSkipAlreadyImported / StatusPendingOwner); Scanner failure surfaces as the right apierr code (DokployUnavailable → yerr.CodeServer, NOT CodeUnavailable — that's for Yalla-owned dependencies); Repository failure preserves catalogued codes; secrets redacted in error strings AND in log buffer (slog over a bytes.Buffer, output.NewRedactor(secret), assert secret never appears); Apply persists in hierarchy order (organization → project → environment → service, asserted by scanning the recorded calls slice for ordering invariants); Apply continues past per-item failures (inject failServiceByDokployID for one specific service create, prove sibling creates still succeeded); Apply ctx-cancellation propagates (errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)); Apply on no-ready-items is a valid no-op accounting Skipped count. The Plan JSON shape is PUBLIC compatibility contract before the customer-facing endpoint even exists — stable field names, no pointers in PlanItem, no embedded interfaces, no time.Time on the wire — because the eventual POST endpoint (BE-0289 et al) serialises it as-is. Canonical test fixture ID format: kind prefix + "_" + 26-char Crockford base32 lowercase suffix from "0123456789abcdefghjkmnpqrstvwxyz" (no i, l, o, u); use "org_01hz00000000000000000000xx" style (4-char prefix + 20 zeros + 2-char tag = 26) — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical for some tests and will fail strict ParseID. Slug derivation goes one-way: domain.NormalizeSlug(dokployName) for the Dokploy → Yalla direction (a NormalizeSlug rejection is itself a classification, NOT an error to propagate); domain.DokployName(label, yallaID) is the OPPOSITE direction and must not be reused here. supportedServiceType(svc) AND-s dokploy.ServiceType.Valid() with the database-requires-engine rule and collapses both failure modes into a single StatusSkipUnsupported reason — the operator's remediation is the same. Reconcile and migrateimport are siblings under this pattern; future "resource lifecycle worker", "drift admin reconciler", "billing reconciler" packages should reuse the package layout verbatim.
