ralph/codex-output/iteration-000093-20260519-061630.log:37799: - Mutating httpapi endpoints follow the writer-port pattern, mirroring the reader-port one: httpapi declares a narrow creator/writer port (e.g. OrganizationCreator), and a store-package unit-of-work orchestrator (OrganizationService, like ProjectService/ServiceAccountService) satisfies it. The orchestrator validates input BEFORE opening a transaction (buildXToCreate is split out + white-box unit-tested with no DB), then composes the desired-state write + audit append inside ONE Store.Write so a created row can never exist without its audit record. The orchestrator audits via *store.AuditRepository directly (it is in the store package — no cycle) and builds the store.AuditEvent from plain-string actor/correlation fields the handler passes in CreateXInput (ActorID/ActorKind/ActorOrgID/RequestID/CorrelationID) — store takes no dependency on policy/telemetry. The audit event is filed under the actor's home org; metadata only carries structurally-safe values (the canonical slug). The handler stays thin: read principal, validate.DecodeJSON the body, pull telemetry.FromContext, call the port, render http.StatusCreated. NewHandler signature is now NewHandler(build, readiness, meta, authenticator, engine, orgs, creator, logger); newRouteTable takes (build, readiness, meta, orgs, creator). Follow-up: mutation audits do not yet capture IP/UA and do not route through audit.Auditor's redactor; denied-decision auditing at the RequireAuth layer is still unwired for every endpoint.
