ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:998:- Audit event metrics follow the existing low-cardinality telemetry collector pattern: `telemetry.AuditEventMetrics` groups by `(action, resource_kind, decision, outcome, reason, error_code)`, stores request/correlation/org/resource/actor/job IDs only as latest-sample incident hints, is emitted from `store.AuditRepository.Append` for both recorded and failed append attempts, and `/metrics` exposes the snapshot under `data.audit_events`.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:1003:- Quota usage metrics follow the existing HTTP/job/Dokploy collector pattern: one in-memory `telemetry.QuotaUsageMetrics` series per low-cardinality `(resource, enforcement_mode, outcome, reason)` tuple, with request/correlation/org/principal/job IDs and quota counts kept only as latest-sample incident hints. The quota checker emits decisions for allowed, rejected, validation, and infrastructure-error paths, and `/metrics` exposes the snapshot under `data.quota_usage`.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:1004:- Dokploy dependency metrics follow the existing HTTP/job queue collector pattern: one in-memory `telemetry.DokployDependencyMetrics` series per low-cardinality `(method, normalized_endpoint, outcome, status_class, error_code, retryable)` tuple; request/correlation/org/project/env/service/principal/job identifiers are latest-sample hints only. The typed Dokploy client emits one event per actual HTTP attempt after local validation, and `/metrics` exposes the snapshot under `data.dokploy_dependencies`.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:1011:- Worker queue metrics follow the HTTP metrics collector shape: aggregate series by low-cardinality dimensions only (`event`, `job_type`, `status`, `status_class`), keep job/request/correlation/org/project/environment/service identifiers as latest-sample log-join hints, and emit worker events only after successful claim/outcome commits so metrics reflect durable queue state.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:1013:- HTTP request metrics live in `internal/controlplane/telemetry` and are wired by `httpapi.NewHandler` inside request logging: group metric series only by method, normalized route, status code, and status class; keep request/correlation/org/principal identifiers as latest-sample log-join hints instead of dashboard labels.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:1127:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, port-driven-pure-classifier instance (BE-0406 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_import_dry_run_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/migrateimport/import_dry_run_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestImportDryRunCoversCallSites + TestImportDryRunPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Import Dry-Run Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: import dry-run tests (BE-0406)" subsection; TWENTY-EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0405 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestImportDryRun ./...`, CI step name `Import dry-run tests`, verify.sh prefix #30 inserted between BE-0405's #29 and the trailing optionals renumbered #30-#34 → #31-#35 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 34 → # 35` and synthetic-fixture `# 33 → # 34` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST port-driven-pure-classifier instance — the chokepoint is `(*migrateimport.Importer).Plan(ctx, PlanInput) (Plan, error)` which is deterministic but reads from port interfaces (Scanner, Repository) rather than being a package-level pure function: (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH IMPORTER PER ITERATION — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE THE CHOKEPOINT TAKES PORT INTERFACES AT CONSTRUCTION TIME` — unlike BE-0405 (which has no shared receiver because Diff is a package-level function), the import-dry-run chokepoint is a method on Importer whose fake Scanner/Repository are scenario-specific. Each goroutine therefore builds its own (Snapshot, fake Repository, Importer) triple per iteration. The burst's value shifts from "package has no shared state" to "the planner's internal pure logic (classify, pendingOwnerItems, proposeSlug, indexProjectsByID, indexEnvironmentsByID, indexServicesByID) has no package-level shared state" — a regression that smuggled in a cached owner table or a sync.Once mutating a per-action map would still surface as a per-iteration mismatch. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-from-ports classifier (`pkg.New(cfg).Method(args)` style): build a fresh chokepoint per iteration; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's pure helpers have no package-level shared state. (2) `THE DRY-RUN PURITY INVARIANT IS A POSITIVE ASSERTION ON THE FAKE REPOSITORY'S RECORDED CALLS, NOT THE ABSENCE OF AN EFFECT — SCAN repo.calls FOR EVERY CREATE PREFIX AFTER EVERY Plan CALL` — the importDryRunCheckOutcome predicate scans the fakeRepo.calls slice for any string with prefix `CreateProject:`, `CreateEnvironment:`, or `CreateService:` and returns a failure when one is found; pending-owner rows additionally assert len(repo.calls) == 0 (the no-owner short-circuit must not touch the repository at all). A regression that started writing through Plan would fail the prefix-scan on its first ready scenario; a regression that started reading the repository under a pending-owner short-circuit would fail the count assertion on its first pending-owner scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is documented read-only on some paths and read-write on others: pin the read-only contract as a positive assertion over the fake's call recorder, not as the absence of an observable side-effect (a state diff would silently pass when the planner reads but does not write, which is itself a regression). (3) `THE PER-ROW REDACTION CANARY ASSERTS MARKER-SURVIVAL IN ProposedDisplay AND MARKER-ABSENCE IN STATUS / REASON / LEVEL — NOT IN ProposedSlug, BECAUSE SLUG IS DERIVED FROM DISPLAY` — the value-free classification contract says the importer's classification surface (Reason, Status, slug, display name) is value-free for sensitive content, but ProposedSlug is itself derived from ProposedDisplay via domain.NormalizeSlug and will deliberately carry alphanumeric content from the human-authored name. The canary therefore seeds importsecretmarker into untrusted Dokploy display names and (a) asserts the marker survives in at least one item's ProposedDisplay (operator review surface MUST render the human name), (b) asserts the marker is absent from string(Level), string(Status), and string(Reason) of every item (closed-set tags MUST stay value-free). Asserting on slug too would over-tighten: a slug derived from a name containing the marker WILL carry the marker by design. Watch out for MaxSlugLen=30 truncation: marker-bearing test names MUST stay ≤ 30 chars so the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug matches the planner's normalized slug exactly (a longer name truncates and the dominance test silently flips to StatusReady). Forward-applicable to every verification story whose value-free contract has a deliberate exception field (display, label, raw-name): enumerate the closed-set tag fields the marker MUST be absent from; do not include the deliberate-exception field in the absence predicate; pin marker survival in the exception field too so the canary's positive direction is also pinned. Two store-layer gotchas worth pinning forward: (A) `domain.ID FIXTURES MUST USE CROCKFORD BASE32 LOWERCASE EXCLUDING i, l, o, u` — the importer's fake repository accepts any string but human-facing IDs in test fixtures follow the canonical Crockford suffix; `proj_01hz00000000000000000000dp` and `proj_01hz00000000000000000000ap` are valid (4 + 20 + 2 = 26 chars), while invented IDs like `proj_…colp` or `proj_…linp` are NOT (they contain `l` or `o`). (B) `NormalizeSlug TRUNCATES TO MaxSlugLen=30 AND TRIMS TRAILING HYPHENS` — keep marker-bearing fixture names short enough that their normalized slug equals their raw input verbatim; the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug uses the same shortened name as the snapshot to avoid silent slug-mismatch drift. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:1137:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, audit-completeness instance (BE-0396 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_audit_completeness_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/audit/audit_completeness_test.go's new canonical pair TestAuditCompletenessCoversCallSites + TestAuditCompletenessPreservesRecordedFieldsUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Audit Completeness Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: audit completeness tests (BE-0396)" subsection; EIGHTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0395 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestAuditCompleteness ./...`, CI step name `Audit completeness tests`, verify.sh prefix #21 inserted between BE-0395's #20 and the trailing optionals renumbered 21->22/22->23/23->24/24->25 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0395's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0396 substring count = 18; matches BE-0395 count — the scope swaps three idempotency-specific anchors `Idempotency-Replayed` / `Idempotency-Key` / `fakeIdempotencyStore` for three audit-specific anchors `Auditor.Record` / `policy.Action` / `policy.Decision`, and swaps `byte-identical` / `E_INVALID_INPUT` / `E_FORBIDDEN` / `E_NOT_FOUND` for `allowed` / `denied` / `concurrentAuditRecorder` / `fakeRecorder`), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0396-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a chokepoint-mediated event-recording surface (audit / metrics / structured-log / outbox emitter) in a Go package with an existing single-call test fixture: (1) `DUAL-FIXTURE REUSE — EXISTING SINGLE-CALL FIXTURE FOR THE COVERAGE MEMBER, A FILE-LOCAL CONCURRENT FIXTURE FOR THE BURST MEMBER` — the canonical pair lives in `package audit_test` (the same external test package as the existing `audit_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `fakeRecorder` / `newAuditor` / `ctxWith` / `orgID` helpers verbatim. The contention burst introduces a SECOND in-file recorder, `concurrentAuditRecorder`, with a mutex-guarded events slice and a `snapshot()` accessor — declaring it in the new test file (not in `audit_test.go`) keeps the existing `fakeRecorder`'s zero-mutex shape for the single-call unit tests that rely on it. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose contention burst would race the existing single-call fixture: don't retrofit a mutex onto the existing helper — add a sibling helper in the new test file. The BE-0395 pattern called for "extend, don't fork" the existing fixtures; BE-0396 refines that to "extend the coverage helper, add a sibling for the contention burst" when the existing helper's zero-mutex shape is part of its contract. (2) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE WALKS ALLOWED *AND* DENIED VARIANTS PER ACTION SURFACE` — the audit-completeness contract is not "every recorded event is well-formed" — it's "every security-relevant decision IS recorded AND well-formed", and denied decisions are as load-bearing as allowed ones (an audit log that omits denials is incomplete by definition). The scenario table therefore enumerates the canonical policy.Action surface (organization.create allowed, project.create allowed, environment.create allowed, service.create allowed, project.delete denied cross-tenant, environment.delete denied out-of-scope, keys.manage denied no-capability, limits.write denied principal-disabled) and the per-scenario `wantDecision` field pins the recorded `store.AuditDecision` to `AuditDecisionAllowed` or `AuditDecisionDenied` so a regression that flipped the recorder's verdict literal trips per-scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose subject is a chokepoint that records BOTH outcomes (audit / metrics counter / structured log emitter / outbox): the closed-set scenario table MUST cover both verdicts, not just the happy path. (3) `PER-EMITTER FIELD-FIDELITY UNDER CONTENTION VIA EXPECTED-SET / OBSERVED-SET DIFF` — the runtime burst seeds `auditCompletenessWorkers * auditCompletenessIterationsPerWorker = 32` concurrent goroutines, each emitting a recorded decision with a unique `req_audit_completeness_burst_<w>_<i>` request_id. After the burst, the test builds two maps (expected: the closed set of synthesized request_ids; observed: the set of request_ids on captured events) and reports both missing-in-observed (a dropped emission OR a cross-write that overwrote one emitter's RequestID with another's — both surface here) AND unexpected-in-observed (a spurious event OR a cross-write that produced a new request_id under contention). Asserting on the SET (not just the count) is what catches the cross-write regression — a recorder that swapped two emitters' RequestIDs under a race would pass the count assertion but fail the set diff. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose contention burst's contract is "every emitter's identifying field is preserved on its recorded event": build expected/observed sets keyed on the emitter-unique identifier, then diff both ways. The burst's auditWorkers=4 × auditIters=8 sizing matches the BE-0395 burst sizing (32 concurrent calls is the smallest power-of-two-burst-table-pair where a non-thread-safe recorder fails under -race ~99% of the time, while keeping the wall-clock under 1s on CI).
