tion so the FIFO fault queue and the recorded-request buffer stay isolated; this avoids the shared-queue contention that would otherwise turn a per-scenario expectedAttempts assertion into a flake under concurrent goroutines. Forward-applicable to every BE-039x chaos-timeout sibling whose invariant is `client classifies X dependency as typed *yerr.Error with Code=Y under fault Z` (BE-0394 chaos Postgres disconnects, future chaos-network-partition / chaos-rate-limit suites). The fixture-table closed-set member's per-scenario knobs — name, method, expectedAttempts, expectedCode, expectedDependency, call closure — are the reusable shape; the BE-0394 Postgres-disconnect sibling extends this by swapping the fake-Dokploy `TimeoutFault` for a fakepg.DisconnectFault and the typed-client surface for the store layer's pgx pool, keeping every other knob.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:1141:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, load-smoke instance (BE-0392 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_load_smoke_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/httpapi/load_smoke_test.go's new canonical pair TestLoadSmokeContractCoversCoreEndpoints + TestLoadSmokeContractRunsBurstWithStableEnvelopes + SECURITY.md "## Load Smoke Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: load smoke tests (BE-0392)" subsection; FOURTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0391 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestLoadSmoke ./...`, CI step name `Load smoke tests`, verify.sh prefix #17 inserted between BE-0390's #16 and the optionals — optionals renumbered #17→#18 govulncheck, #18→#19 staticcheck, #19→#20 golangci-lint, #20→#21 goreleaser in the SAME edit; TENTH consecutive required-step insertion with optional renumbering since BE-0380's #6). Two BE-0392-specific extensions to the template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose load-bearing subject is an HTTP handler under burst load: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR ENCODES DETERMINISTIC × DETERMINISTIC DUAL — NO POSTGRES MEMBER` — unlike BE-0389/BE-0390 whose canonical pair splits across deterministic-fixture-only + Postgres-required substrates, BE-0392's BOTH pair members are deterministic. The bootstrap surface (`/healthz`, `/readyz`, `/version`) is the one part of the public HTTP API that needs no backing infrastructure — no Postgres, no fake Dokploy fixtures, no quota counters mutated, no scope-resolution chain. The closed-set member walks an in-memory endpoint table; the burst member spins the test handler in-process via httptest.NewServer and fires 16 workers × 32 iterations = 512 concurrent requests per endpoint. Neither member calls t.Skip on missing env vars — the gate trips on every developer machine and every CI runner without setup. Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a stateless HTTP surface or a pure-in-process invariant (BE-0407 desired-state goldens against in-memory reconciler, BE-0398 tenant isolation matrix at the policy layer alone). Critical implication: the BE-0389/BE-0390 dual-substrate shape is NOT mandatory for the canonical-pair template — when both invariants are deterministic, the pair can stay deterministic-only and the SECURITY.md section's "skips when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset" language is OMITTED (BE-0392's section instead explicitly says "no Postgres, no live Dokploy server"). The shared `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY` opt-in-external substring stays in the substring set as the cross-suite escape hatch reference, but does NOT bind to any code path the canonical pair member exercises. (2) `BURST OBSERVATION COLLECTION SHAPE — channel buffered to total request count, observation struct includes endpoint + status + schema + ok + requestID + bodyBad + headerBad, then post-burst aggregation in main goroutine` — the burst test must avoid testing.T calls from worker goroutines (race-detector hostile and stdlib-discouraged); instead each goroutine pushes a typed `observation{}` onto a buffered channel sized to `len(endpoints) * workers * iterations` and the main goroutine drains the channel after wg.Wait()+close. Aggregation builds (a) a per-endpoint count map asserted against the expected burst total, (b) a per-request-id map for uniqueness assertion, (c) per-observation envelope/status/redaction assertions. Failure messages quote the offending endpoint AND the observed request_id (or "<missing>" sentinel + a synthetic "<request-construction-failed:...>" / "<request-do-failed:...>" id when the round-trip itself failed) so the operator correlates against logs without re-running. The burst total (16 × 32 × 3 = 1536 requests) completes in ~600ms on a developer laptop — safely under the flaky-test threshold. Forward-applicable to BE-0393 (chaos Dokploy timeouts — same channel+aggregation shape, but with a fault-injection step before each iteration), BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — same shape but with t.Skip on YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL), BE-0395 (idempotency replay — same shape but each iteration writes the same Idempotency-Key and asserts every response after the first is the cached envelope). The closed-set bootstrap endpoint table includes a `wantStatus` field per entry so a future endpoint that legitimately returns 503 under some condition can be added without breaking the burst member's status assertion. Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0390's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0392 substring count = 17; matches BE-0389's count), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:1219:- Operations artefact verification leaf (BE-0409 pattern, internal/controlplane/build/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, api_dockerfile_test.go}; structural template for BE-0410 worker Dockerfile, BE-0411 compose, BE-0412 k8s/Helm, BE-0413 systemd, BE-0414+ migration / seed-admin / backup / restore commands, BE-0418 config example files): canonical shape for ANY operations artefact (Dockerfile, docker-compose, k8s manifest, systemd unit, deploy script) the backend ships at the repo root. One Go test file per artefact in this package (api_dockerfile_test.go, worker_dockerfile_test.go, compose_test.go, ...), package `build_test` (external _test package) so the verification surface has no production-code dependency. Tests locate the artefact via repoRoot(t) which walks up from os.Getwd() until it finds go.mod — hard-coded relative paths break when the test moves. The verification surface is structural (regex over Dockerfile text, set-membership over .dockerignore lines, YAML parse + key lookup for compose/k8s) rather than a real `docker build` because the audit properties operations review cares about (non-root user, pinned tags, no baked secrets, EXPOSE matches the production listen address from config.ProfileProduction's apiAddr default) are textual, not behavioural. Forbidden-secret list MUST track config.Config's secret fields (DatabaseURL, SigningKeys, DokployToken) plus the most common database-side secret (POSTGRES_PASSWORD); extend it in lockstep when future config stories add new secret fields. ENV NAME=VAL, legacy `ENV NAME VAL`, and `ARG NAME=VAL with non-empty default` are ALL three banned forms — a developer who pastes `ARG YALLA_DOKPLOY_TOKEN=local-dev-token` into a Dockerfile fails this test in CI. Distroless static-debian12:nonroot is the canonical runtime image for any CGO_ENABLED=0 Go binary in this repo (UID/GID 65532, no shell, no package manager); HEALTHCHECK is intentionally omitted because the image has no curl/wget/sh — orchestrators probe /healthz and /readyz over HTTP directly. The BuildKit cache mounts (`--mount=type=cache,target=/go/pkg/mod` and `/root/.cache/go-build`) require the `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7` directive at the top of the file; without that line the syntax silently falls back to legacy and the cache mounts become no-ops. GO_VERSION and RUNTIME_IMAGE are ARGs with pinned defaults so a downstream operator can override them without editing the file — and the verification test asserts the ARG defaults themselves are not :latest. The .dockerignore is documented as defence-in-depth, not an authorization boundary (secrets must never be checked in regardless); the required-exclusion set tested includes .git, .env, secrets/, *.pem, *.key. EXPOSE port is pinned to 8080 to match ProfileStaging/ProfileProduction's apiAddr default in internal/controlplane/config/load.go — drift here would force operators to read the source to know which port to map.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:1224:- Operator-driven health probe (BE-0039 pattern, internal/controlplane/backup/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, health.go, health_test.go} + httpapi/routes.go GET /healthz/backup + docs/operations/backup-restore.md): canonical shape for ANY unauthenticated probe that surfaces operator-written state the Yalla process did not produce itself — database backup timestamp, WAL position, replication lag, external queue depth, cloud-bucket presence, certificate expiry, etc. Layout: a LEAF package under internal/controlplane that owns the parsing + redaction contract (Status struct + Reporter interface + a concrete FileReporter and an Unconfigured() helper); a single new entry in newRouteTable with tagOperations + a stable operationId (getHealthzBackup mirrors getHealthz/getReadyz/getVersion); a wire-DTO struct with omitempty pointers for every optional field so the four wire-shape variants (configured-and-healthy / configured-and-stale / configured-but-empty-sentinel / unconfigured) share one shape. Five load-bearing rules: (1) the leaf reads exactly one external resource — a path or URL the operator supplied via a YALLA_* env var; never reads the database, never invokes Dokploy, never logs the source content; (2) error messages from the leaf name the configured path or url but never echo the source's content (a status file accidentally seeded with a secret cannot leak through the leaf's parse-error string, pinned by a TestFileReporterParseErrorRedactsContent-style test); (3) the HTTP handler never echoes the reporter's error message in the wire response either — it forwards a stable handler-side message via apienvelope.WriteErrorStatus(yerr.CodeUnavailable, ...) and the redaction proof lives in a TestXxxErrorMessageDoesNotLeakReporterError test on the http side; (4) a sentinel "configured but no value yet" path (ErrNoBackupRecorded shape) renders 200 with configured=true + fresh=false + a human-readable detail string — a freshly provisioned environment is healthy, it just hasn't fired its first event yet; (5) an unconfigured Reporter renders 200 with configured=false (NOT 404) so operators can wire the integration later without restarting clients that already probe the route. The four BE-acceptance-criteria slots map onto the new endpoint as: success = source returns a value; validation failure = wrong HTTP method (router-driven 404/405; the BE-0039 "no input to validate" version of a validation test); authorization failure = explicit unauthenticated-with-and-without-bearer-header test that fails loudly if a future regression wraps the route in RequireAuth; not-found behavior = the sentinel path (configured but no value yet recorded). Config layer gains TWO env vars per probe — a YALLA_XXX_PATH/URL (string, optional, must be absolute when set) and a YALLA_XXX_MAX_AGE (Go duration, optional, ≥ 0) — with Validate rejecting a relative path and the RedactedConfig+LogValue+String chain surfacing the non-secret fields. NewHandler grows ONE positional port (Reporter backup.Reporter or similar) at the head of the operational-args block (after meta, before authenticator); a nil Reporter is rewritten to Unconfigured() inside NewHandler so every test path still serves the route. The positional-arg sweep across ~60 test files is dominated by three Python regexes anchored on the common test helpers (`nil, nil, a, policy.NewEngine()` literal-a, `nil, nil, AuthIdent{}, policy.NewEngine()` struct-literal-auth, multi-line `nil, nil, a,\n\t\tpolicy.NewEngine()`); the all-nils newRouteTable shape tests bump by one nil; the bootstrap-endpoints list test in server_test.go adds the new path. The runbook in docs/operations/ is part of the deliverable — it pins the SLO (frequency, retention, RTO/RPO, encryption), the operator-pipeline handshake (the exact file format + atomic-rename write contract), the four wire-shape variants the probe emits, and the rehearsal procedure for staging. Forward-applicable to every future operator-driven health probe (queue depth, replication lag, certificate expiry, cloud-bucket presence, archive freshness, license validity, KMS key health).
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:1226:- Pure orchestrator / workflow package = reconcile-shaped vertical (BE-0033 pattern, internal/controlplane/migrateimport/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, types.go, importer.go, importer_test.go}, the second instance of this shape after internal/controlplane/reconcile): canonical shape for ANY workflow that ingests external state, classifies it, and writes through ports — Dokploy import, drift reconciliation, billing reconciliation, compliance scanning, lifecycle teardown, etc. Package layout: doc.go (boundary + plan/apply + dry-run + redaction sections), AGENTS.md (conventions), types.go (every port interface PLUS every value type those interfaces reference — Scanner/Repository ports, Snapshot/Existing*/Create*Input value types, OwnerAssignment, PlanItem and Plan with stable JSON tags because the Plan IS the dry-run yalla.output.v1 payload; ItemStatus + ItemReason as the closed classification surface), importer.go (Config with Scanner + Repository + Logger + Now + Redactor; New() returns apierr.Internal on a missing port; Plan(ctx, in) is pure and never writes; Apply(ctx, plan) is best-effort and collects per-item failures in Result.Failures with redacted error strings; classify*() helpers are unit-testable in isolation; wrap{Scanner,Repository}Err() preserves catalogued apierr.* codes and upgrades untyped errors to apierr.DokployUnavailable (scanner) / apierr.StoreUnavailable (repository) / apierr.Internal (defensive contract assertions); a resolvedParents map seeded with the org-root entry lets the apply loop resolve child-after-parent in a single pass through the hierarchy-ordered items). The test file houses an in-package fakeScanner + fakeRepo with concurrent-safe mutexes and call recording, and covers: construction port validation (one nil per case → CodeInternal); Plan input validation (every required field, every kind/format check → CodeInvalidInput with the named field recoverable via apierr.ViolationsOf); the dry-run-doesn't-query-Repository contract (no-assignment Plan never calls the repo); every classification Status × Reason combination as a positive case (StatusReady / StatusSkipDuplicate / StatusSkipMissingParent / StatusSkipUnsupported / StatusSkipAlreadyImported / StatusPendingOwner); Scanner failure surfaces as the right apierr code (DokployUnavailable → yerr.CodeServer, NOT CodeUnavailable — that's for Yalla-owned dependencies); Repository failure preserves catalogued codes; secrets redacted in error strings AND in log buffer (slog over a bytes.Buffer, output.NewRedactor(secret), assert secret never appears); Apply persists in hierarchy order (organization → project → environment → service, asserted by scanning the recorded calls slice for ordering invariants); Apply continues past per-item failures (inject failServiceByDokployID for one specific service create, prove sibling creates still succeeded); Apply ctx-cancellation propagates (errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)); Apply on no-ready-items is a valid no-op accounting Skipped count. The Plan JSON shape is PUBLIC compatibility contract before the customer-facing endpoint even exists — stable field names, no pointers in PlanItem, no embedded interfaces, no time.Time on the wire — because the eventual POST endpoint (BE-0289 et al) serialises it as-is. Canonical test fixture ID format: kind prefix + "_" + 26-char Crockford base32 lowercase suffix from "0123456789abcdefghjkmnpqrstvwxyz" (no i, l, o, u); use "org_01hz00000000000000000000xx" style (4-char prefix + 20 zeros + 2-char tag = 26) — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical for some tests and will fail strict ParseID. Slug derivation goes one-way: domain.NormalizeSlug(dokployName) for the Dokploy → Yalla direction (a NormalizeSlug rejection is itself a classification, NOT an error to propagate); domain.DokployName(label, yallaID) is the OPPOSITE direction and must not be reused here. supportedServiceType(svc) AND-s dokploy.ServiceType.Valid() with the database-requires-engine rule and collapses both failure modes into a single StatusSkipUnsupported reason — the operator's remediation is the same. Reconcile and migrateimport are siblings under this pattern; future "resource lifecycle worker", "drift admin reconciler", "billing reconciler" packages should reuse the package layout verbatim.
