ralph/codex-output/iteration-000099-20260519-070549.log:1229:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, port-driven-pure-classifier instance (BE-0406 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_import_dry_run_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/migrateimport/import_dry_run_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestImportDryRunCoversCallSites + TestImportDryRunPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Import Dry-Run Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: import dry-run tests (BE-0406)" subsection; TWENTY-EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0405 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestImportDryRun ./...`, CI step name `Import dry-run tests`, verify.sh prefix #30 inserted between BE-0405's #29 and the trailing optionals renumbered #30-#34 → #31-#35 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 34 → # 35` and synthetic-fixture `# 33 → # 34` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST port-driven-pure-classifier instance — the chokepoint is `(*migrateimport.Importer).Plan(ctx, PlanInput) (Plan, error)` which is deterministic but reads from port interfaces (Scanner, Repository) rather than being a package-level pure function: (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH IMPORTER PER ITERATION — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE THE CHOKEPOINT TAKES PORT INTERFACES AT CONSTRUCTION TIME` — unlike BE-0405 (which has no shared receiver because Diff is a package-level function), the import-dry-run chokepoint is a method on Importer whose fake Scanner/Repository are scenario-specific. Each goroutine therefore builds its own (Snapshot, fake Repository, Importer) triple per iteration. The burst's value shifts from "package has no shared state" to "the planner's internal pure logic (classify, pendingOwnerItems, proposeSlug, indexProjectsByID, indexEnvironmentsByID, indexServicesByID) has no package-level shared state" — a regression that smuggled in a cached owner table or a sync.Once mutating a per-action map would still surface as a per-iteration mismatch. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-from-ports classifier (`pkg.New(cfg).Method(args)` style): build a fresh chokepoint per iteration; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's pure helpers have no package-level shared state. (2) `THE DRY-RUN PURITY INVARIANT IS A POSITIVE ASSERTION ON THE FAKE REPOSITORY'S RECORDED CALLS, NOT THE ABSENCE OF AN EFFECT — SCAN repo.calls FOR EVERY CREATE PREFIX AFTER EVERY Plan CALL` — the importDryRunCheckOutcome predicate scans the fakeRepo.calls slice for any string with prefix `CreateProject:`, `CreateEnvironment:`, or `CreateService:` and returns a failure when one is found; pending-owner rows additionally assert len(repo.calls) == 0 (the no-owner short-circuit must not touch the repository at all). A regression that started writing through Plan would fail the prefix-scan on its first ready scenario; a regression that started reading the repository under a pending-owner short-circuit would fail the count assertion on its first pending-owner scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is documented read-only on some paths and read-write on others: pin the read-only contract as a positive assertion over the fake's call recorder, not as the absence of an observable side-effect (a state diff would silently pass when the planner reads but does not write, which is itself a regression). (3) `THE PER-ROW REDACTION CANARY ASSERTS MARKER-SURVIVAL IN ProposedDisplay AND MARKER-ABSENCE IN STATUS / REASON / LEVEL — NOT IN ProposedSlug, BECAUSE SLUG IS DERIVED FROM DISPLAY` — the value-free classification contract says the importer's classification surface (Reason, Status, slug, display name) is value-free for sensitive content, but ProposedSlug is itself derived from ProposedDisplay via domain.NormalizeSlug and will deliberately carry alphanumeric content from the human-authored name. The canary therefore seeds importsecretmarker into untrusted Dokploy display names and (a) asserts the marker survives in at least one item's ProposedDisplay (operator review surface MUST render the human name), (b) asserts the marker is absent from string(Level), string(Status), and string(Reason) of every item (closed-set tags MUST stay value-free). Asserting on slug too would over-tighten: a slug derived from a name containing the marker WILL carry the marker by design. Watch out for MaxSlugLen=30 truncation: marker-bearing test names MUST stay ≤ 30 chars so the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug matches the planner's normalized slug exactly (a longer name truncates and the dominance test silently flips to StatusReady). Forward-applicable to every verification story whose value-free contract has a deliberate exception field (display, label, raw-name): enumerate the closed-set tag fields the marker MUST be absent from; do not include the deliberate-exception field in the absence predicate; pin marker survival in the exception field too so the canary's positive direction is also pinned. Two store-layer gotchas worth pinning forward: (A) `domain.ID FIXTURES MUST USE CROCKFORD BASE32 LOWERCASE EXCLUDING i, l, o, u` — the importer's fake repository accepts any string but human-facing IDs in test fixtures follow the canonical Crockford suffix; `proj_01hz00000000000000000000dp` and `proj_01hz00000000000000000000ap` are valid (4 + 20 + 2 = 26 chars), while invented IDs like `proj_…colp` or `proj_…linp` are NOT (they contain `l` or `o`). (B) `NormalizeSlug TRUNCATES TO MaxSlugLen=30 AND TRIMS TRAILING HYPHENS` — keep marker-bearing fixture names short enough that their normalized slug equals their raw input verbatim; the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug uses the same shortened name as the snapshot to avoid silent slug-mismatch drift. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000099-20260519-070549.log:1239:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, audit-completeness instance (BE-0396 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_audit_completeness_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/audit/audit_completeness_test.go's new canonical pair TestAuditCompletenessCoversCallSites + TestAuditCompletenessPreservesRecordedFieldsUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Audit Completeness Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: audit completeness tests (BE-0396)" subsection; EIGHTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0395 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestAuditCompleteness ./...`, CI step name `Audit completeness tests`, verify.sh prefix #21 inserted between BE-0395's #20 and the trailing optionals renumbered 21->22/22->23/23->24/24->25 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0395's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0396 substring count = 18; matches BE-0395 count — the scope swaps three idempotency-specific anchors `Idempotency-Replayed` / `Idempotency-Key` / `fakeIdempotencyStore` for three audit-specific anchors `Auditor.Record` / `policy.Action` / `policy.Decision`, and swaps `byte-identical` / `E_INVALID_INPUT` / `E_FORBIDDEN` / `E_NOT_FOUND` for `allowed` / `denied` / `concurrentAuditRecorder` / `fakeRecorder`), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0396-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a chokepoint-mediated event-recording surface (audit / metrics / structured-log / outbox emitter) in a Go package with an existing single-call test fixture: (1) `DUAL-FIXTURE REUSE — EXISTING SINGLE-CALL FIXTURE FOR THE COVERAGE MEMBER, A FILE-LOCAL CONCURRENT FIXTURE FOR THE BURST MEMBER` — the canonical pair lives in `package audit_test` (the same external test package as the existing `audit_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `fakeRecorder` / `newAuditor` / `ctxWith` / `orgID` helpers verbatim. The contention burst introduces a SECOND in-file recorder, `concurrentAuditRecorder`, with a mutex-guarded events slice and a `snapshot()` accessor — declaring it in the new test file (not in `audit_test.go`) keeps the existing `fakeRecorder`'s zero-mutex shape for the single-call unit tests that rely on it. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose contention burst would race the existing single-call fixture: don't retrofit a mutex onto the existing helper — add a sibling helper in the new test file. The BE-0395 pattern called for "extend, don't fork" the existing fixtures; BE-0396 refines that to "extend the coverage helper, add a sibling for the contention burst" when the existing helper's zero-mutex shape is part of its contract. (2) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE WALKS ALLOWED *AND* DENIED VARIANTS PER ACTION SURFACE` — the audit-completeness contract is not "every recorded event is well-formed" — it's "every security-relevant decision IS recorded AND well-formed", and denied decisions are as load-bearing as allowed ones (an audit log that omits denials is incomplete by definition). The scenario table therefore enumerates the canonical policy.Action surface (organization.create allowed, project.create allowed, environment.create allowed, service.create allowed, project.delete denied cross-tenant, environment.delete denied out-of-scope, keys.manage denied no-capability, limits.write denied principal-disabled) and the per-scenario `wantDecision` field pins the recorded `store.AuditDecision` to `AuditDecisionAllowed` or `AuditDecisionDenied` so a regression that flipped the recorder's verdict literal trips per-scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose subject is a chokepoint that records BOTH outcomes (audit / metrics counter / structured log emitter / outbox): the closed-set scenario table MUST cover both verdicts, not just the happy path. (3) `PER-EMITTER FIELD-FIDELITY UNDER CONTENTION VIA EXPECTED-SET / OBSERVED-SET DIFF` — the runtime burst seeds `auditCompletenessWorkers * auditCompletenessIterationsPerWorker = 32` concurrent goroutines, each emitting a recorded decision with a unique `req_audit_completeness_burst_<w>_<i>` request_id. After the burst, the test builds two maps (expected: the closed set of synthesized request_ids; observed: the set of request_ids on captured events) and reports both missing-in-observed (a dropped emission OR a cross-write that overwrote one emitter's RequestID with another's — both surface here) AND unexpected-in-observed (a spurious event OR a cross-write that produced a new request_id under contention). Asserting on the SET (not just the count) is what catches the cross-write regression — a recorder that swapped two emitters' RequestIDs under a race would pass the count assertion but fail the set diff. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose contention burst's contract is "every emitter's identifying field is preserved on its recorded event": build expected/observed sets keyed on the emitter-unique identifier, then diff both ways. The burst's auditWorkers=4 × auditIters=8 sizing matches the BE-0395 burst sizing (32 concurrent calls is the smallest power-of-two-burst-table-pair where a non-thread-safe recorder fails under -race ~99% of the time, while keeping the wall-clock under 1s on CI).
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000099-20260519-070549.log:1242:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, chaos-Dokploy-timeouts instance (BE-0393 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_chaos_dokploy_timeouts_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/chaos_dokploy_timeouts_test.go's new canonical pair TestChaosDokployTimeoutsCoversCallSites + TestChaosDokployTimeoutsMapsToTypedTimeoutEnvelope + SECURITY.md "## Chaos Tests For Dokploy Timeouts" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: chaos tests for Dokploy timeouts (BE-0393)" subsection; FIFTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0392 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestChaosDokployTimeouts ./...`, CI step name `Chaos tests for Dokploy timeouts`, verify.sh prefix #18 inserted between BE-0392's #17 and the trailing optionals renumbered 18->19/19->20/20->21/21->22 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0392's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0393 substring count = 16; one fewer than BE-0392 because the chaos suite is package-bounded to internal/controlplane/dokploy and does not need a separate /healthz//readyz//version path enumeration — instead it names `TimeoutFault`, `yerr.CodeTimeout`, and `apierr.DependencyDokploy` as the load-bearing classification anchors), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Additional pattern member specific to chaos-classification verification stories: the canonical pair member that runs the runtime burst constructs a FRESH fake-Dokploy server per iteration (not per goroutine) via runChaosIteration so the FIFO fault queue and the recorded-request buffer stay isolated; this avoids the shared-queue contention that would otherwise turn a per-scenario expectedAttempts assertion into a flake under concurrent goroutines. Forward-applicable to every BE-039x chaos-timeout sibling whose invariant is `client classifies X dependency as typed *yerr.Error with Code=Y under fault Z` (BE-0394 chaos Postgres disconnects, future chaos-network-partition / chaos-rate-limit suites). The fixture-table closed-set member's per-scenario knobs — name, method, expectedAttempts, expectedCode, expectedDependency, call closure — are the reusable shape; the BE-0394 Postgres-disconnect sibling extends this by swapping the fake-Dokploy `TimeoutFault` for a fakepg.DisconnectFault and the typed-client surface for the store layer's pgx pool, keeping every other knob.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000099-20260519-070549.log:1243:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, load-smoke instance (BE-0392 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_load_smoke_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/httpapi/load_smoke_test.go's new canonical pair TestLoadSmokeContractCoversCoreEndpoints + TestLoadSmokeContractRunsBurstWithStableEnvelopes + SECURITY.md "## Load Smoke Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: load smoke tests (BE-0392)" subsection; FOURTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0391 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestLoadSmoke ./...`, CI step name `Load smoke tests`, verify.sh prefix #17 inserted between BE-0390's #16 and the optionals — optionals renumbered #17→#18 govulncheck, #18→#19 staticcheck, #19→#20 golangci-lint, #20→#21 goreleaser in the SAME edit; TENTH consecutive required-step insertion with optional renumbering since BE-0380's #6). Two BE-0392-specific extensions to the template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose load-bearing subject is an HTTP handler under burst load: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR ENCODES DETERMINISTIC × DETERMINISTIC DUAL — NO POSTGRES MEMBER` — unlike BE-0389/BE-0390 whose canonical pair splits across deterministic-fixture-only + Postgres-required substrates, BE-0392's BOTH pair members are deterministic. The bootstrap surface (`/healthz`, `/readyz`, `/version`) is the one part of the public HTTP API that needs no backing infrastructure — no Postgres, no fake Dokploy fixtures, no quota counters mutated, no scope-resolution chain. The closed-set member walks an in-memory endpoint table; the burst member spins the test handler in-process via httptest.NewServer and fires 16 workers × 32 iterations = 512 concurrent requests per endpoint. Neither member calls t.Skip on missing env vars — the gate trips on every developer machine and every CI runner without setup. Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a stateless HTTP surface or a pure-in-process invariant (BE-0407 desired-state goldens against in-memory reconciler, BE-0398 tenant isolation matrix at the policy layer alone). Critical implication: the BE-0389/BE-0390 dual-substrate shape is NOT mandatory for the canonical-pair template — when both invariants are deterministic, the pair can stay deterministic-only and the SECURITY.md section's "skips when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset" language is OMITTED (BE-0392's section instead explicitly says "no Postgres, no live Dokploy server"). The shared `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY` opt-in-external substring stays in the substring set as the cross-suite escape hatch reference, but does NOT bind to any code path the canonical pair member exercises. (2) `BURST OBSERVATION COLLECTION SHAPE — channel buffered to total request count, observation struct includes endpoint + status + schema + ok + requestID + bodyBad + headerBad, then post-burst aggregation in main goroutine` — the burst test must avoid testing.T calls from worker goroutines (race-detector hostile and stdlib-discouraged); instead each goroutine pushes a typed `observation{}` onto a buffered channel sized to `len(endpoints) * workers * iterations` and the main goroutine drains the channel after wg.Wait()+close. Aggregation builds (a) a per-endpoint count map asserted against the expected burst total, (b) a per-request-id map for uniqueness assertion, (c) per-observation envelope/status/redaction assertions. Failure messages quote the offending endpoint AND the observed request_id (or "<missing>" sentinel + a synthetic "<request-construction-failed:...>" / "<request-do-failed:...>" id when the round-trip itself failed) so the operator correlates against logs without re-running. The burst total (16 × 32 × 3 = 1536 requests) completes in ~600ms on a developer laptop — safely under the flaky-test threshold. Forward-applicable to BE-0393 (chaos Dokploy timeouts — same channel+aggregation shape, but with a fault-injection step before each iteration), BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — same shape but with t.Skip on YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL), BE-0395 (idempotency replay — same shape but each iteration writes the same Idempotency-Key and asserts every response after the first is the cached envelope). The closed-set bootstrap endpoint table includes a `wantStatus` field per entry so a future endpoint that legitimately returns 503 under some condition can be added without breaking the burst member's status assertion. Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0390's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0392 substring count = 17; matches BE-0389's count), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000099-20260519-070549.log:1321:- Operations artefact verification leaf (BE-0409 pattern, internal/controlplane/build/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, api_dockerfile_test.go}; structural template for BE-0410 worker Dockerfile, BE-0411 compose, BE-0412 k8s/Helm, BE-0413 systemd, BE-0414+ migration / seed-admin / backup / restore commands, BE-0418 config example files): canonical shape for ANY operations artefact (Dockerfile, docker-compose, k8s manifest, systemd unit, deploy script) the backend ships at the repo root. One Go test file per artefact in this package (api_dockerfile_test.go, worker_dockerfile_test.go, compose_test.go, ...), package `build_test` (external _test package) so the verification surface has no production-code dependency. Tests locate the artefact via repoRoot(t) which walks up from os.Getwd() until it finds go.mod — hard-coded relative paths break when the test moves. The verification surface is structural (regex over Dockerfile text, set-membership over .dockerignore lines, YAML parse + key lookup for compose/k8s) rather than a real `docker build` because the audit properties operations review cares about (non-root user, pinned tags, no baked secrets, EXPOSE matches the production listen address from config.ProfileProduction's apiAddr default) are textual, not behavioural. Forbidden-secret list MUST track config.Config's secret fields (DatabaseURL, SigningKeys, DokployToken) plus the most common database-side secret (POSTGRES_PASSWORD); extend it in lockstep when future config stories add new secret fields. ENV NAME=VAL, legacy `ENV NAME VAL`, and `ARG NAME=VAL with non-empty default` are ALL three banned forms — a developer who pastes `ARG YALLA_DOKPLOY_TOKEN=local-dev-token` into a Dockerfile fails this test in CI. Distroless static-debian12:nonroot is the canonical runtime image for any CGO_ENABLED=0 Go binary in this repo (UID/GID 65532, no shell, no package manager); HEALTHCHECK is intentionally omitted because the image has no curl/wget/sh — orchestrators probe /healthz and /readyz over HTTP directly. The BuildKit cache mounts (`--mount=type=cache,target=/go/pkg/mod` and `/root/.cache/go-build`) require the `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7` directive at the top of the file; without that line the syntax silently falls back to legacy and the cache mounts become no-ops. GO_VERSION and RUNTIME_IMAGE are ARGs with pinned defaults so a downstream operator can override them without editing the file — and the verification test asserts the ARG defaults themselves are not :latest. The .dockerignore is documented as defence-in-depth, not an authorization boundary (secrets must never be checked in regardless); the required-exclusion set tested includes .git, .env, secrets/, *.pem, *.key. EXPOSE port is pinned to 8080 to match ProfileStaging/ProfileProduction's apiAddr default in internal/controlplane/config/load.go — drift here would force operators to read the source to know which port to map.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000099-20260519-070549.log:1326:- Operator-driven health probe (BE-0039 pattern, internal/controlplane/backup/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, health.go, health_test.go} + httpapi/routes.go GET /healthz/backup + docs/operations/backup-restore.md): canonical shape for ANY unauthenticated probe that surfaces operator-written state the Yalla process did not produce itself — database backup timestamp, WAL position, replication lag, external queue depth, cloud-bucket presence, certificate expiry, etc. Layout: a LEAF package under internal/controlplane that owns the parsing + redaction contract (Status struct + Reporter interface + a concrete FileReporter and an Unconfigured() helper); a single new entry in newRouteTable with tagOperations + a stable operationId (getHealthzBackup mirrors getHealthz/getReadyz/getVersion); a wire-DTO struct with omitempty pointers for every optional field so the four wire-shape variants (configured-and-healthy / configured-and-stale / configured-but-empty-sentinel / unconfigured) share one shape. Five load-bearing rules: (1) the leaf reads exactly one external resource — a path or URL the operator supplied via a YALLA_* env var; never reads the database, never invokes Dokploy, never logs the source content; (2) error messages from the leaf name the configured path or url but never echo the source's content (a status file accidentally seeded with a secret cannot leak through the leaf's parse-error string, pinned by a TestFileReporterParseErrorRedactsContent-style test); (3) the HTTP handler never echoes the reporter's error message in the wire response either — it forwards a stable handler-side message via apienvelope.WriteErrorStatus(yerr.CodeUnavailable, ...) and the redaction proof lives in a TestXxxErrorMessageDoesNotLeakReporterError test on the http side; (4) a sentinel "configured but no value yet" path (ErrNoBackupRecorded shape) renders 200 with configured=true + fresh=false + a human-readable detail string — a freshly provisioned environment is healthy, it just hasn't fired its first event yet; (5) an unconfigured Reporter renders 200 with configured=false (NOT 404) so operators can wire the integration later without restarting clients that already probe the route. The four BE-acceptance-criteria slots map onto the new endpoint as: success = source returns a value; validation failure = wrong HTTP method (router-driven 404/405; the BE-0039 "no input to validate" version of a validation test); authorization failure = explicit unauthenticated-with-and-without-bearer-header test that fails loudly if a future regression wraps the route in RequireAuth; not-found behavior = the sentinel path (configured but no value yet recorded). Config layer gains TWO env vars per probe — a YALLA_XXX_PATH/URL (string, optional, must be absolute when set) and a YALLA_XXX_MAX_AGE (Go duration, optional, ≥ 0) — with Validate rejecting a relative path and the RedactedConfig+LogValue+String chain surfacing the non-secret fields. NewHandler grows ONE positional port (Reporter backup.Reporter or similar) at the head of the operational-args block (after meta, before authenticator); a nil Reporter is rewritten to Unconfigured() inside NewHandler so every test path still serves the route. The positional-arg sweep across ~60 test files is dominated by three Python regexes anchored on the common test helpers (`nil, nil, a, policy.NewEngine()` literal-a, `nil, nil, AuthIdent{}, policy.NewEngine()` struct-literal-auth, multi-line `nil, nil, a,\n\t\tpolicy.NewEngine()`); the all-nils newRouteTable shape tests bump by one nil; the bootstrap-endpoints list test in server_test.go adds the new path. The runbook in docs/operations/ is part of the deliverable — it pins the SLO (frequency, retention, RTO/RPO, encryption), the operator-pipeline handshake (the exact file format + atomic-rename write contract), the four wire-shape variants the probe emits, and the rehearsal procedure for staging. Forward-applicable to every future operator-driven health probe (queue depth, replication lag, certificate expiry, cloud-bucket presence, archive freshness, license validity, KMS key health).
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000099-20260519-070549.log:1328:- Pure orchestrator / workflow package = reconcile-shaped vertical (BE-0033 pattern, internal/controlplane/migrateimport/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, types.go, importer.go, importer_test.go}, the second instance of this shape after internal/controlplane/reconcile): canonical shape for ANY workflow that ingests external state, classifies it, and writes through ports — Dokploy import, drift reconciliation, billing reconciliation, compliance scanning, lifecycle teardown, etc. Package layout: doc.go (boundary + plan/apply + dry-run + redaction sections), AGENTS.md (conventions), types.go (every port interface PLUS every value type those interfaces reference — Scanner/Repository ports, Snapshot/Existing*/Create*Input value types, OwnerAssignment, PlanItem and Plan with stable JSON tags because the Plan IS the dry-run yalla.output.v1 payload; ItemStatus + ItemReason as the closed classification surface), importer.go (Config with Scanner + Repository + Logger + Now + Redactor; New() returns apierr.Internal on a missing port; Plan(ctx, in) is pure and never writes; Apply(ctx, plan) is best-effort and collects per-item failures in Result.Failures with redacted error strings; classify*() helpers are unit-testable in isolation; wrap{Scanner,Repository}Err() preserves catalogued apierr.* codes and upgrades untyped errors to apierr.DokployUnavailable (scanner) / apierr.StoreUnavailable (repository) / apierr.Internal (defensive contract assertions); a resolvedParents map seeded with the org-root entry lets the apply loop resolve child-after-parent in a single pass through the hierarchy-ordered items). The test file houses an in-package fakeScanner + fakeRepo with concurrent-safe mutexes and call recording, and covers: construction port validation (one nil per case → CodeInternal); Plan input validation (every required field, every kind/format check → CodeInvalidInput with the named field recoverable via apierr.ViolationsOf); the dry-run-doesn't-query-Repository contract (no-assignment Plan never calls the repo); every classification Status × Reason combination as a positive case (StatusReady / StatusSkipDuplicate / StatusSkipMissingParent / StatusSkipUnsupported / StatusSkipAlreadyImported / StatusPendingOwner); Scanner failure surfaces as the right apierr code (DokployUnavailable → yerr.CodeServer, NOT CodeUnavailable — that's for Yalla-owned dependencies); Repository failure preserves catalogued codes; secrets redacted in error strings AND in log buffer (slog over a bytes.Buffer, output.NewRedactor(secret), assert secret never appears); Apply persists in hierarchy order (organization → project → environment → service, asserted by scanning the recorded calls slice for ordering invariants); Apply continues past per-item failures (inject failServiceByDokployID for one specific service create, prove sibling creates still succeeded); Apply ctx-cancellation propagates (errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)); Apply on no-ready-items is a valid no-op accounting Skipped count. The Plan JSON shape is PUBLIC compatibility contract before the customer-facing endpoint even exists — stable field names, no pointers in PlanItem, no embedded interfaces, no time.Time on the wire — because the eventual POST endpoint (BE-0289 et al) serialises it as-is. Canonical test fixture ID format: kind prefix + "_" + 26-char Crockford base32 lowercase suffix from "0123456789abcdefghjkmnpqrstvwxyz" (no i, l, o, u); use "org_01hz00000000000000000000xx" style (4-char prefix + 20 zeros + 2-char tag = 26) — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical for some tests and will fail strict ParseID. Slug derivation goes one-way: domain.NormalizeSlug(dokployName) for the Dokploy → Yalla direction (a NormalizeSlug rejection is itself a classification, NOT an error to propagate); domain.DokployName(label, yallaID) is the OPPOSITE direction and must not be reused here. supportedServiceType(svc) AND-s dokploy.ServiceType.Valid() with the database-requires-engine rule and collapses both failure modes into a single StatusSkipUnsupported reason — the operator's remediation is the same. Reconcile and migrateimport are siblings under this pattern; future "resource lifecycle worker", "drift admin reconciler", "billing reconciler" packages should reuse the package layout verbatim.
